  "thumbnailAlt": "Grant Application form beside a photo of a nonprofit team working together in an office",
  "content": {
    "intro": "A grant application form is a form that collects an applicant's organization details, a project description, the amount requested, a budget, timeline and expected outcomes so a funder can assess and compare requests. This template follows the order most review panels read in, from who is applying to what the money will change, and takes the budget as an upload.",
    "audience": "Community foundations, family foundations, local councils, corporate giving programs and nonprofits that re-grant funds.",
    "useCases": [
      "An annual community grants round for local charities",
      "A small-grants program for neighborhood projects under $5,000",
      "A corporate foundation's employee-nominated grants",
      "An arts council's project funding call"
    ],
    "tips": [
      "Applicants can save progress and resume, which matters for long narrative answers they draft over several days.",
      "Set an opening and closing time in Form Settings to match your grant round exactly.",
      "Add your review panel as collaborators and use the response analytics to compare focus areas and amounts requested across the round."
    ],
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "What should a grant application form include?",
        "answer": "Applicant organization name, legal status and registration or tax ID; contact person; project title and summary; the need being addressed; activities and timeline; amount requested and total project cost; a budget; other funding sources; measurable outcomes; and a certification that the information is accurate."
      },
      {
        "question": "How long should grant application answers be?",
        "answer": "Short answers are easier to compare. Many funders cap the summary at 100 to 200 words and each narrative section at 300 to 500 words; set a character limit on each answer to match."
      },
      {
        "question": "Should I accept applications from individuals?",
        "answer": "That depends on your funding rules. Some funders can only grant to registered charities or tax-exempt groups, so check your own rules and local law and adjust the organization type options."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I review grant applications as a panel?",
        "answer": "Export applications to Excel or CSV and score each against a shared rubric such as need, feasibility, outcomes and value for money. Collaborators can view responses in the same form."
      }
    ]
  },
